# The archive is the product. A Defold consumer depends on this repository by URL
# to an archive of a ref, and Bob resolves that archive by taking the *first* zip
# entry named game.project as the library root and reading [library] include_dirs
# from it. GitHub emits archive entries in git tree order, so any Defold project
# living under a directory that sorts before the root game.project silently
# becomes that root instead - Bob then mounts no include dirs and the consumer
# fails with "the Lua module '/yes2sdk/yes2sdk.lua' can't be found", an error that
# says nothing about packaging. ci/consumer/ is exactly such a project, and it
# broke the edge lane on its first real run. CI scaffolding is not part of what an
# integrator downloads, so it is not archived - which also leaves exactly one
# game.project in the archive, so entry order stops mattering at all.
# ci/verify-dependency-archive.mjs asserts that outcome on every pull request.
/ci/ export-ignore
